  "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There is the only value $m=0$ such that $m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there is the only value $m=0$ such that $m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={\u2026,-\\pi ,0,\\pi ,2\\pi ,\u2026}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"",
